## Working agreements

- Read the relevant code and instructions before editing. State the assumptions that change behavior or scope.
- Make the smallest change that completes the request. Reuse existing patterns, components and dependencies.
- Preserve local changes, validation, error handling, security, accessibility and explicit requirements.
- Do not add abstractions, options, dependencies or features nobody asked for.
- Run the checks that cover the change. Test relevant logic and regressions. A trivial change does not need a test that only mirrors the code.
- Keep "file present", "configured" and "integration tested" separate. Do not report an assumption as a fact or a pending integration as a result.
- Continue authorized work. Ask only for essential facts or for actions outside the authorization.
- Publishing, messaging third parties, spending money and destructive actions need their own authorization. Installing a tool does not grant it.

## Continuity

- A blocker stops only the work that depends on it. Finish the independent work that stays safe and authorized.
- Do not repeat an identical attempt without new evidence. Respect cost, time and retry limits and earlier refusals.
- When resuming, check the real files, Git state, checks and processes. A summary from an earlier session does not prove the current state.
- For work that spans sessions, keep a short handoff in `.gifisi/handoff.md`, written from `{{HARNESS_HOME}}core/templates/task-handoff.md.template`.

## Standards

- Source files stay at or below 350 lines, counted without blank and comment lines. Split on a real responsibility seam, never at an arbitrary line.
- Validate input and environment at the boundary. Log on the server through the project logger, without secrets or full request bodies.
- Presentation code calls feature modules; it does not import the database client.
- Preserve the existing stack, database and public contracts. A migration is separate, explicitly requested work.
- Update the document that owns a fact in the same change: `PRODUCT.md` for scope, `docs/architecture.md` for structure, `docs/design.md` for interface, `docs/onboarding.md` for preparation, `docs/quality.md` for checks, `docs/setup.md` for tools and evidence.
